    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "During the COVID-19 pandemic, the federal government enacted $2.2 trillion in fund- ing, a proven method of stimulating economic activity during recessions. However, the pandemic-induced recession was relatively shorter and more severe than all other economic recessions. The Paycheck Protection Program (PPP) provided approximately $800 billion in uncollateralized loans, almost all of which were forgiven. Current literature has established the differential loan distribution due to private banks distributing public loans and analyzed the effect on job loss and company payroll expenses. How- ever, there is a gap in the literature in understanding how the PPP loans affected all business outcomes at a granular level. In this paper, I analyze the effect of the distribution of PPP loans and amounts amongst California businesses at the zip code and industry level. Using the California Department of Tax and Fee Administration’s (CDTFA) business-level administrative data, I find that zip codes with higher shares of businesses receiving PPP loans had a lower likelihood of business closures, particularly when the zip code experiences higher shares of businesses with declines in taxable sales. These effects are augmented for industries that experienced larger employment contractions in the early stage of the pandemic.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Hou, E. (2024). Did the Paycheck Protection Program Reduce Business Closures? Evidence from California. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/sm210zt7969. https://doi.org/10.25740/sm210zt7969.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
